BEGIN;

DELETE FROM questions WHERE topic = 'Embryogenesis & Development' AND subtopic IN ('Blastulation', 'Cleavage', 'Fertilization', 'Implantation & Extraembryonic Membranes', 'Twins');

-- Q1 [Fertilization | easy | Skill 1 | ans A]
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'Embryogenesis & Development', $sub1$Fertilization$sub1$, $qt1$At the instant the first sperm binds and fuses with a human oocyte's plasma membrane, the oocyte triggers an immediate, electrical first line of defense that blocks any additional sperm from fusing within about a minute, well before slower changes to the egg's coat occur. This near-instantaneous fast block to polyspermy is produced by which event?$qt1$, $op1$[{"label": "A", "text": "A sudden rise in sodium-ion permeability depolarizes the oocyte's plasma membrane, preventing additional sperm from fusing with it."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Cortical granules beneath the oocyte's membrane fuse with it and release zonal inhibiting proteins into the space outside the membrane."}, {"label": "C", "text": "A calcium-ion influx hardens the zona pellucida into an impenetrable fertilization membrane surrounding the new zygote."}, {"label": "D", "text": "Acrosomal digestive enzymes are released that degrade the glycoprotein matrix of the zona pellucida around the oocyte."}]$op1$::jsonb, 'A', $ex1$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Early Embryonic Development'. The answer is A because the fast block to polyspermy is a near-instantaneous change in sodium-ion permeability that depolarizes the oocyte plasma membrane the moment the first sperm binds. The text states that "the fast block...involves a near instantaneous change in sodium ion permeability upon binding of the first sperm, depolarizing the oocyte plasma membrane and preventing the fusion of additional sperm cells," and that this block "sets in almost immediately and lasts for about a minute." This electrical depolarization is the immediate first line of defense and is distinct from the slower cortical reaction, which is driven by a calcium influx and takes place over the following minute. (Choice A) is correct because the rapid increase in sodium-ion permeability depolarizes the membrane and is the fast block itself. (Choice B) describes the cortical reaction—the slow block—in which cortical granules release zonal inhibiting proteins, not the fast electrical block. (Choice C) describes a downstream outcome of the slow block, in which mucopolysaccharides and a hardened zona pellucida form the fertilization membrane, not the immediate depolarization. (Choice D) describes the acrosomal reaction, which lets a sperm INTO the oocyte rather than keeping additional sperm out. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because it requires recalling the defined mechanism and ionic basis of the fast block to polyspermy directly from the text.$ex1$, 'easy', '2C', $cc1$Early Embryonic Development$cc1$, 'biology', 1, 75
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'B', 'process_step_confusion', $a1B$Substitutes the slow-block cortical reaction step for the asked-about fast-block step$a1B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'adjacent_fact', $a1C$Names a real downstream slow-block outcome (fertilization membrane) instead of the immediate depolarization$a1C$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'reversed_relationship', $a1D$Cites the acrosomal reaction that admits sperm, reversing block-out into let-in$a1D$ FROM q;

-- Q2 [Fertilization | hard | Skill 2 | ans B]
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'Embryogenesis & Development', $sub2$Fertilization$sub2$, $qt2$An investigator fertilizes oocytes in a medium that clamps the oocyte's sodium-ion permeability, so the plasma membrane cannot change its membrane potential in the first seconds after a sperm binds; the post-penetration calcium-ion influx and all granule exocytosis remain fully intact. Within the first few seconds after the first sperm binds, which protective response is the oocyte now unable to mount?$qt2$, $op2$[{"label": "A", "text": "The cortical reaction, in which granules beneath the membrane discharge zonal inhibiting proteins that destroy the sperm-binding receptors."}, {"label": "B", "text": "The fast block, in which a change in sodium permeability depolarizes the membrane and prevents additional sperm from fusing."}, {"label": "C", "text": "The slow block, in which mucopolysaccharides coat the zygote and harden the zona into a fertilization membrane."}, {"label": "D", "text": "The acrosomal reaction, in which the sperm cap discharges digestive enzymes that clear a path through the zona pellucida."}]$op2$::jsonb, 'B', $ex2$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Early Embryonic Development'. The answer is B because the fast block to polyspermy is precisely a near-instantaneous change in sodium-ion permeability that depolarizes the oocyte plasma membrane the moment the first sperm binds; clamping sodium permeability so the membrane cannot depolarize is exactly what abolishes this response. The text states the fast block "involves a near instantaneous change in sodium ion permeability upon binding of the first sperm, depolarizing the oocyte plasma membrane and preventing the fusion of additional sperm cells." Because the scenario leaves the calcium influx and granule exocytosis untouched, the calcium-triggered slow block (the cortical reaction and the mucopolysaccharide-built fertilization membrane) can still proceed; only the sodium-dependent electrical fast block, which alone acts within the first few seconds, is lost. (Choice A) The cortical reaction is the slow block and is triggered by the intact calcium influx, so it still occurs and is not the lost response. (Choice B) is correct because the fast block is the sodium-permeability change that depolarizes the membrane in the first seconds, and that is the step the sodium clamp specifically prevents. (Choice C) The mucopolysaccharide coating that hardens the zona into a fertilization membrane is part of the granule-driven slow block, which remains functional because calcium and exocytosis are intact. (Choice D) The acrosomal reaction is a sperm event that discharges enzymes to breach the zona before fusion, not an oocyte polyspermy block, and it is unrelated to the oocyte's sodium permeability. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because it requires predicting, from an experimental manipulation that isolates the sodium-dependent step while sparing the calcium-dependent steps, which specific polyspermy block is selectively eliminated.$ex2$, 'hard', '2C', $cc2$Early Embryonic Development$cc2$, 'biology', 2, 95
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'adjacent_fact', $a2A$Names the calcium-triggered cortical reaction (the slow block) instead of the sodium-dependent fast block, ignoring that calcium and exocytosis are explicitly preserved$a2A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'partial_truth', $a2C$Correctly describes a real polyspermy block (the mucopolysaccharide/fertilization-membrane slow block) but it is the calcium-dependent step that the manipulation leaves intact$a2C$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'process_step_confusion', $a2D$Substitutes the sperm's pre-fusion acrosomal reaction for the oocyte's post-binding sodium-dependent fast block$a2D$ FROM q;

-- Q3 [Fertilization | hard | Skill 2 | ans C]
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'Embryogenesis & Development', $sub3$Fertilization$sub3$, $qt3$A researcher draws blood separately from the single umbilical vein and from the two umbilical arteries of a healthy near-term human fetus and measures the oxygen content of each sample. Predict which vessel carries the more oxygen-rich blood and the direction that blood is moving.$qt3$, $op3$[{"label": "A", "text": "The two umbilical arteries carry the oxygen-rich blood and deliver it toward the fetus, while the umbilical vein returns oxygen-depleted blood toward the placenta."}, {"label": "B", "text": "The umbilical vein carries oxygen-poor blood toward the fetal liver, where the blood is first oxygenated before that oxygen-rich blood is returned toward the placenta in the arteries."}, {"label": "C", "text": "The umbilical vein carries the oxygen-rich blood and delivers it toward the fetus, while the two umbilical arteries return oxygen-depleted blood toward the placenta."}, {"label": "D", "text": "The umbilical vein carries oxygen-rich blood toward the fetus, and the two umbilical arteries also carry oxygen-rich blood as they move that same blood toward the placenta."}]$op3$::jsonb, 'C', $ex3$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Early Embryonic Development'. The answer is C because at the placenta the umbilical vein picks up freshly oxygenated blood from the maternal side and carries it to the fetus, while the umbilical arteries carry the fetus's spent, oxygen-depleted blood back to the placenta. The text states that "Emerging from the placenta is the umbilical vein, which carries oxygen-rich blood from the pregnant person to the fetal inferior vena cava... to the heart that pumps it into fetal circulation," whereas "Two umbilical arteries carry oxygen-depleted fetal blood, including wastes and carbon dioxide, to the placenta." This reverses the usual postnatal rule that arteries carry oxygenated blood: because the placenta, not the lungs, is the organ of gas exchange before birth, it is the vein leaving the placenta that is oxygen-rich and the arteries leaving the fetus that are oxygen-poor. (Choice A) is incorrect because it swaps the roles of the two vessels: the oxygen-rich blood is in the umbilical vein moving toward the fetus, not in the arteries. (Choice B) is incorrect because the umbilical vein already carries oxygen-rich blood that it acquired at the placenta; the ductus venosus actually lets this blood "bypass the fetal liver and go directly to the fetal heart," so the liver does not oxygenate it and the arteries do not carry it back oxygen-rich. (Choice C) is correct because the umbilical vein is the oxygen-rich vessel flowing to the fetus and the umbilical arteries are the oxygen-depleted vessels flowing to the placenta. (Choice D) is incorrect because, although the vein is correctly described, the umbilical arteries carry oxygen-depleted blood (wastes and carbon dioxide) rather than oxygen-rich blood to the placenta. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because it requires predicting measured oxygen content in named vessels by reasoning from the placenta's role as the fetal gas-exchange organ rather than recalling the postnatal artery/vein convention.$ex3$, 'hard', '2C', $cc3$Early Embryonic Development$cc3$, 'biology', 2, 105
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'reversed_relationship', $a3A$Applying the postnatal rule that arteries carry oxygenated blood, reversing the actual oxygenation and flow direction of the umbilical vein and arteries$a3A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'B', 'process_step_confusion', $a3B$Inserting the fetal liver as the site of oxygenation and confusing the ductus venosus bypass, when the umbilical vein is already oxygen-rich from the placenta and the liver is bypassed$a3B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'partial_truth', $a3D$Correctly identifying the umbilical vein as oxygen-rich toward the fetus but wrongly making the umbilical arteries also oxygen-rich instead of oxygen-depleted$a3D$ FROM q;

-- Q4 [Fertilization | medium | Skill 2 | ans D]
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'Embryogenesis & Development', $sub4$Fertilization$sub4$, $qt4$In a fertilization assay, an oocyte's fast block and cortical (slow) block both fail, and two separate sperm successfully fuse with and contribute their nuclei to the single oocyte. A researcher tracks the resulting cell to determine the genetic content of the zygote and the fate of the embryo it produces.$qt4$, $op4$[{"label": "A", "text": "The zygote is diploid and develops normally, because only one of the two sperm nuclei is ever permitted to combine with the egg nucleus."}, {"label": "B", "text": "The zygote becomes haploid and arrests, because the extra sperm strips one chromosome set out of the egg during fusion."}, {"label": "C", "text": "The zygote is tetraploid with four sets and develops into identical twins, because each sperm independently drives a full cleavage program."}, {"label": "D", "text": "The zygote is triploid with three chromosome sets, a condition incompatible with life, so the embryo is not genetically viable and dies within days."}]$op4$::jsonb, 'D', $ex4$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Early Embryonic Development'. The answer is D because the polyspermy blocks normally exist precisely to prevent more than one sperm from fertilizing the oocyte; when both blocks fail and two sperm contribute their haploid nuclei to one haploid oocyte, the cell receives three haploid chromosome sets. The Anatomy & Physiology text states that "if more than one sperm were to fertilize the oocyte, the resulting zygote would be a triploid organism with three sets of chromosomes. This is incompatible with life," and the Biology text adds that when these mechanisms fail "the resulting embryo is not genetically viable and dies within a few days." A normal fertilization joins one sperm pronucleus with one ovum pronucleus to yield a diploid zygote, so adding a second sperm genome produces an excess (three sets), not a deficiency or a normal count. (Choice A) is wrong because once both blocks have failed there is no remaining mechanism to exclude the second sperm's genome, so the second set is not screened out and the cell does not stay diploid. (Choice B) is wrong because added sperm contribute genomes rather than removing the egg's set; the lesion here is excess chromosomal material, not a haploid deficiency. (Choice C) is wrong because two sperm plus one egg sum to three sets, not four, and polyspermy causes inviability rather than twinning. (Choice D) is correct because three combined haploid sets make the zygote triploid, a state the texts label incompatible with life, dooming the embryo within days. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because it requires predicting the chromosomal count and downstream viability outcome from the failure of a regulatory mechanism rather than recalling a stated definition.$ex4$, 'medium', '2C', $cc4$Early Embryonic Development$cc4$, 'biology', 2, 105
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'misconception', $a4A$Belief that a backup selection step still excludes the second genome even after both blocks have failed$a4A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'B', 'reversed_relationship', $a4B$Treats the extra sperm as subtracting a chromosome set (deficiency) rather than adding one (excess)$a4B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'scale_unit_error', $a4C$Miscounts the chromosome sets (2 sperm + 1 egg = 3, not 4) and conflates polyspermy with twinning$a4C$ FROM q;

-- Q5 [Twins | easy | Skill 1 | ans B]
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'Embryogenesis & Development', $sub5$Twins$sub5$, $qt5$A pregnant patient's two fetuses are confirmed to have developed from two eggs released in the same cycle, each fertilized by a different sperm. A genetic counselor predicts how genetically similar these twins will be to one another.$qt5$, $op5$[{"label": "A", "text": "They are genetically identical, because both arose from one fertilized egg that split into two embryos."}, {"label": "B", "text": "They are no more genetically alike than ordinary siblings born in separate pregnancies."}, {"label": "C", "text": "They share exactly half their genes, because one egg was fertilized by two different sperm."}, {"label": "D", "text": "They carry different chromosome numbers, because the original zygote misdivided during its first cleavage."}]$op5$::jsonb, 'B', $ex5$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Early Embryonic Development'. The answer is B because the scenario describes the defining mechanism of dizygotic (fraternal) twinning: two separately ovulated eggs are each fertilized by a different sperm, producing two genetically distinct zygotes. The A&P 2e text states that "two eggs are released and both are fertilized. Two zygotes form, implant, and develop, resulting in the birth of dizygotic (or fraternal) twins," and that "because dizygotic twins develop from two eggs fertilized by two sperm, they are no more identical than siblings born at different times" (L57500-57502). Because each zygote receives an independent random combination of the parents' alleles, the twins are no more similar than any pair of full siblings. (Choice A) is the monozygotic (identical) mechanism, in which a single zygote "can divide into two separate offspring during early development," producing twins from one fertilized egg, not two (L57507-57508). (Choice B) is correct because two eggs plus two sperm yield two independent zygotes whose genetic similarity equals that of ordinary siblings. (Choice C) describes polyspermy, which is normally prevented by the cortical reaction's slow block (L57480-57486); fraternal twinning requires two separate eggs rather than one egg fertilized twice. (Choice D) misattributes the outcome to a cleavage error, but cleavage divisions are ordinary mitoses that produce blastomeres without changing chromosome number (L57602-57604). This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because it requires recalling the established mechanism by which dizygotic twins arise and its direct consequence for their genetic relatedness.$ex5$, 'easy', '2C', $cc5$Early Embryonic Development$cc5$, 'biology', 1, 75
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'reversed_relationship', $a5A$Swaps the dizygotic mechanism for the monozygotic (identical) one — one zygote splitting into two embryos$a5A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'misconception', $a5C$Assumes one egg can be fertilized by two sperm, ignoring the cortical reaction's polyspermy block$a5C$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'adjacent_fact', $a5D$Attributes twinning to a cleavage/nondisjunction error, treating mitotic cleavage as if it alters ploidy$a5D$ FROM q;

-- Q6 [Twins | medium | Skill 2 | ans C]
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'Embryogenesis & Development', $sub6$Twins$sub6$, $qt6$A genetic-counseling lab sequences nuclear DNA from two same-sex children born in the same delivery and finds their sequences essentially indistinguishable from each other. Based on this finding alone, a counselor predicts the zygotic origin of the pair. Which prediction is best supported?$qt6$, $op6$[{"label": "A", "text": "They are dizygotic, because fraternal twins develop from one shared zygote and therefore carry matching nuclear DNA."}, {"label": "B", "text": "They must be dizygotic, because two children of the same biological sex can only have arisen as fraternal twins."}, {"label": "C", "text": "They are monozygotic, because a near-identical nuclear DNA sequence is expected only when both arose from a single zygote that later divided."}, {"label": "D", "text": "They are monozygotic only if they also shared one placenta in utero, regardless of how alike their nuclear DNA is."}]$op6$::jsonb, 'C', $ex6$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Early Embryonic Development'. The answer is C because an essentially identical nuclear genome between two siblings is the genetic signature of a single zygote that split into two offspring. The text states that the genes in a person's chromosomes "are unique for each individual except identical twins" (L3724), so matching nuclear DNA points specifically to identical (monozygotic) twins, which arise when "a zygote can divide into two separate offspring during early development" (L57507-57508). Dizygotic twins, by contrast, "develop from two eggs fertilized by two sperm" and are "no more identical than siblings born at different times" (L57501-57502), so they would not show matching sequences. Placenta count reflects only the timing of the split (two-cell vs. blastocyst stage, L57510-57511) and does not define zygosity, which is set by whether the children trace to one zygote. (Choice A) is reversed: dizygotic (fraternal) twins come from two separate zygotes and are no more alike than ordinary siblings, not from one shared zygote. (Choice B) is wrong because same-sex pairing does not establish zygosity; dizygotic twins are simply ordinary siblings genetically and can be the same sex. (Choice C) is correct because the text ties a unique genome to "identical twins" and identical twins to a single dividing zygote. (Choice D) is wrong because a shared placenta indicates a late (blastocyst-stage) split but the defining criterion of monozygosity is the identical nuclear DNA, not the number of placentas. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because it requires using a DNA-sequence observation to infer the embryonic origin of the twins by applying the genetic-identity rules that distinguish monozygotic from dizygotic twinning.$ex6$, 'medium', '2C', $cc6$Early Embryonic Development$cc6$, 'biology', 2, 105
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'reversed_relationship', $a6A$Swaps the origin of dizygotic twins, claiming fraternal twins come from one shared zygote and so have matching DNA$a6A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'B', 'adjacent_fact', $a6B$Uses a true but irrelevant fact (same biological sex) as if it determined zygosity$a6B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'partial_truth', $a6D$Correctly notes placenta-sharing relates to twinning but wrongly makes placenta count, not DNA, the defining criterion of monozygosity$a6D$ FROM q;

-- Q7 [Twins | medium | Skill 2 | ans D]
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'Embryogenesis & Development', $sub7$Twins$sub7$, $qt7$A monozygotic cleavage event separates a single conceptus into two independent embryos while it is still at the two-cell stage, several days before it would reach the uterus. A clinician imaging the resulting pregnancy predicts how many placentas the twins will have.$qt7$, $op7$[{"label": "A", "text": "The twins will share a single placenta within one common chorionic cavity."}, {"label": "B", "text": "The twins will be fraternal, each developing within its own separate placenta."}, {"label": "C", "text": "The split will fail because neither cell yet contains a placenta-forming trophoblast."}, {"label": "D", "text": "The twins will be identical, each developing within its own separate placenta."}]$op7$::jsonb, 'D', $ex7$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Early Embryonic Development'. The answer is D because a zygote that splits as early as the two-cell stage produces monozygotic (identical) twins whose embryos each go on to build an individual placenta. The text states that "the zygote can split as early as the two-cell stage" and that "the twin embryos that separated at the two-cell stage will have individual placentas," whereas only embryos that separate later, at the blastocyst stage, "share a placenta and a chorionic cavity" (A&P 2e L57508–57511). Splitting at this point precedes blastocyst formation, when the outer trophoblast cells that become the fetal portion of the placenta first differentiate (L57611–57613), so each separated cell can later form its own trophoblast and therefore its own placenta. Because the offspring arise from one zygote, they remain monozygotic regardless of when the split occurs; the timing changes the membranes, not the zygosity. (Choice A) Sharing a single placenta and one chorionic cavity is the outcome of a later split at the blastocyst stage, not a two-cell-stage split. (Choice B) Splitting one zygote yields monozygotic, identical twins, not fraternal ones; fraternal twins require two separately fertilized eggs. (Choice C) A two-cell-stage split still yields viable twins with individual placentas even though the trophoblast has not yet differentiated, so the split does not fail. (Choice D) An early two-cell-stage separation gives identical twins that each build a separate placenta, matching the text. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because it requires predicting the placentation outcome by reasoning from the timing of the split relative to when the placenta-forming trophoblast differentiates, rather than recalling a single stated fact.$ex7$, 'medium', '2C', $cc7$Early Embryonic Development$cc7$, 'biology', 2, 105
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'process_step_confusion', $a7A$Applies the outcome of a later blastocyst-stage split (shared placenta and chorionic cavity) to an early two-cell-stage split.$a7A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'B', 'misconception', $a7B$Believes an early split produces non-identical (fraternal) twins, conflating split timing with zygosity.$a7B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'adjacent_fact', $a7C$Correctly notes the trophoblast differentiates only at the blastocyst stage but wrongly concludes an earlier split is nonviable.$a7C$ FROM q;

-- Q8 [Twins | hard | Skill 2 | ans A]
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'Embryogenesis & Development', $sub8$Twins$sub8$, $qt8$A pair of genetically identical twins is delivered sharing a single chorionic sac and a single amniotic sac. To produce this monochorionic-monoamniotic arrangement, the developmental split of the embryo must have occurred at which point relative to membrane formation?$qt8$, $op8$[{"label": "A", "text": "After the trophoblast had organized one chorionic sac and after the amnion had begun to form, so both membranes were already shared structures when the embryo divided"}, {"label": "B", "text": "At the two-cell stage, before any extra-embryonic membranes had formed, so each embryo built its own chorion and amnion that later happened to abut"}, {"label": "C", "text": "From two separate eggs that implanted side by side, whose two amniotic sacs subsequently fused into one shared cavity"}, {"label": "D", "text": "After the chorion split into two but before the amnion divided, because the amnion is the outermost membrane enclosing all the others"}]$op8$::jsonb, 'A', $ex8$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Early Embryonic Development'. The answer is A because a single shared chorion and a single shared amnion can only arise if the inner cell mass divides after both of those membranes have already been laid down as single structures. The trophoblast (outer shell of the blastocyst) develops into the chorionic sac, and the amnion forms from the epiblast during the second week of development; a split that occurs only after the chorionic sac is organized and the amnion has begun forming leaves both extra-embryonic membranes as common, shared compartments enclosing both embryos. The text confirms that twins separating at the blastocyst stage "share a placenta and a chorionic cavity," and pushing the split even later, past amnion formation, is what additionally makes the amnion shared. (Choice A) is correct: the later the split, the more membranes are already established and therefore shared, so a monochorionic-monoamniotic pair reflects the latest split, after both chorion and amnion are formed. (Choice B) is wrong because a two-cell-stage split is the earliest split and precedes all membrane formation, so "twin embryos that separated at the two-cell stage will have individual placentas"—each twin builds its own chorion and amnion (dichorionic-diamniotic), the opposite of the shared arrangement described. (Choice C) describes dizygotic twins, which arise when "two eggs are released and both are fertilized," forming two genetically distinct zygotes; that scenario cannot create a single true amnion and is excluded here because the twins are genetically identical. (Choice D) reverses the membrane geometry: the chorion, not the amnion, is "the one membrane that surrounds all others," so naming the amnion as the outermost enclosing membrane misidentifies which membrane is outer and gives an incoherent splitting order. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because it requires reasoning backward from an observed shared-membrane outcome to infer the relative timing of the embryonic split against the sequence of trophoblast-derived chorion and epiblast-derived amnion formation.$ex8$, 'hard', '2C', $cc8$Early Embryonic Development$cc8$, 'biology', 2, 160
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'B', 'reversed_relationship', $a8B$Treating the earliest split as the cause of shared membranes when it actually produces individual (separate) membranes$a8B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'misconception', $a8C$Conflating monozygotic shared-membrane twins with dizygotic twins formed from two separate fertilized eggs$a8C$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'reversed_relationship', $a8D$Swapping which extra-embryonic membrane is outermost, naming the amnion instead of the chorion as the membrane that surrounds all others$a8D$ FROM q;

-- Q9 [Twins | hard | Skill 2 | ans B]
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'Embryogenesis & Development', $sub9$Twins$sub9$, $qt9$A pair of newborn twins are genetically identical, share a single placenta, and are physically joined at the chest. A developmental biologist is asked to reconstruct the single event that produced this outcome. Which event best accounts for both the genetic identity and the physical joining of these twins?$qt9$, $op9$[{"label": "A", "text": "Two separately fertilized eggs implanted so close together that their growing embryonic discs fused into a single shared body."}, {"label": "B", "text": "One zygote began dividing into two embryos so late that its single inner cell mass separated only partially, leaving the offspring joined."}, {"label": "C", "text": "One zygote split cleanly at the two-cell stage, and the two embryos later grew together into a shared placenta and merged."}, {"label": "D", "text": "One zygote split completely at the blastocyst stage, after which one twin's tissues were partially resorbed by the other."}]$op9$::jsonb, 'B', $ex9$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Early Embryonic Development'. The answer is B because genetically identical twins arise from a single zygote that divides into two offspring, and the text specifies that this split occurs latest at the early blastocyst stage; if that single inner cell mass divides incompletely, the two embryos remain joined while staying genetically identical and sharing one placenta. The text states that "a zygote can divide into two separate offspring during early development," producing monozygotic (identical) twins, and that "splitting occurs most commonly during the early blastocyst stage" (L57507-57509). It also notes that "twin embryos that form from separation at the blastocyst stage will share a placenta and a chorionic cavity" (L57510-57511), matching the shared placenta seen here; physical joining follows from that late split being incomplete rather than clean. (Choice A) is wrong because two separately fertilized eggs are dizygotic—"two eggs fertilized by two sperm"—and are "no more identical than siblings" (L57501-57502), so they cannot be genetically identical. (Choice B) is correct because a single late-splitting zygote whose inner cell mass divides only partially yields identical, joined twins sharing one placenta. (Choice C) is wrong because a two-cell-stage split gives twins with "individual placentas" (L57510) and fully separate bodies, not a shared placenta or joining from a later merger. (Choice D) is wrong because a complete blastocyst-stage split produces separate twins sharing one placenta (L57510-57511); joining requires the split itself to be incomplete, not later resorption. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because it requires chaining the latest split timing to the incomplete separation of one inner cell mass to explain both the genetic identity and the physical joining of the twins.$ex9$, 'hard', '2C', $cc9$Early Embryonic Development$cc9$, 'biology', 2, 160
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
