UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'adjacent_fact', $a12C$Imports the twinning capability of separated indeterminate blastomeres into a determinate scenario where fates are fixed$a12C$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'misconception', $a12D$Invents a replacement-by-extra-division mechanism, ignoring that cleavage involves no growth and fixed-fate cells cannot be substituted$a12D$ FROM q;

-- Q13 [Cleavage | hard | Skill 2 | ans B]
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'Embryogenesis & Development', $sub13$Cleavage$sub13$, $qt13$A developmental biologist working with an early sea urchin embryo, whose cells are not yet committed to specific fates, uses a fine glass needle to split the blastomeres into two separate groups and cultures each group independently. She then predicts the developmental outcome of the two groups.$qt13$, $op13$[{"label": "A", "text": "Only the group retaining the original animal pole forms a complete embryo, while the other group arrests because it lacks the determinants for the missing structures."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Each group develops into a complete embryo, yielding two genetically identical organisms, because the uncommitted blastomeres can each generate every cell type."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Each group develops into a half-embryo missing complementary structures, so the separation produces conjoined rather than fully separate twins."}, {"label": "D", "text": "Neither group develops, because separating the blastomeres destroys the cell-to-cell signaling each blastomere needs to maintain its already-assigned fate."}]$op13$::jsonb, 'B', $ex13$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Early Embryonic Development'. The answer is B because the sea urchin (a deuterostome) undergoes indeterminate cleavage, in which the blastomeres "are not yet fully committed at this early stage to develop into specific cell types," so each separated group retains the capacity to build a whole organism. The text states that "Removal of individual blastomeres from these embryos does not result in the loss of embryonic structures," and that "twins (clones) can be produced as a result from blastomeres that have been separated from the original mass of blastomere cells"; because both groups derive from the same zygote, the result is two genetically identical organisms—the same mechanism the Anatomy & Physiology text describes for monozygotic (identical) twins, where "a zygote can divide into two separate offspring during early development." Since the cells are uncommitted, no group is missing fate-restricted determinants and none arrests. (Choice A) is wrong because indeterminate cleavage means no single group monopolizes the determinants for a complete embryo—removal of blastomeres "does not result in the loss of embryonic structures," so the smaller group does not arrest. (Choice B) is correct because uncommitted blastomeres can each regenerate every cell type, producing two whole, genetically identical embryos. (Choice C) describes the wrong cleavage type: half-embryos missing complementary structures would follow determinate cleavage, where "Removal of a blastomere... can result in missing structures, and embryos that fail to develop," not the indeterminate cleavage of this embryo. (Choice D) reverses the relationship, asserting fixed fates and signal-dependent maintenance, but indeterminate-cleavage cells have no "already-assigned fate" to lose. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because it requires applying the property of indeterminate cleavage to a novel experimental manipulation and predicting the outcome of a physical separation rather than recalling a definition.$ex13$, 'hard', '2C', $cc13$Early Embryonic Development$cc13$, 'biology', 2, 150
  ) RETURNING id
)
