UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'adjacent_fact', $a13C$Applies the determinate-cleavage outcome (half-embryos / missing complementary structures) to an indeterminate-cleavage embryo$a13C$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'reversed_relationship', $a13D$Reverses the commitment relationship by assuming blastomeres already have assigned, signal-dependent fates that separation destroys$a13D$ FROM q;

-- Q14 [Cleavage | hard | Skill 2 | ans C]
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'Embryogenesis & Development', $sub14$Cleavage$sub14$, $qt14$During the first several cleavage divisions, a deuterostome zygote is partitioned into progressively more blastomeres while the embryo as a whole stays the same overall size. A developmental biologist asks what this geometry does to each blastomere's surface-area-to-volume ratio and what advantage it confers on the early embryo.$qt14$, $op14$[{"label": "A", "text": "Each blastomere's surface-area-to-volume ratio falls, because dividing the cytoplasm concentrates it into a more compact mass with proportionally less exposed surface."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Each blastomere's surface-area-to-volume ratio is unchanged, because both the membrane surface and the cytoplasm are halved together at every division."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Each blastomere's surface-area-to-volume ratio rises, giving the embryo more membrane exchange surface per unit of cytoplasm even though total embryo volume is unchanged."}, {"label": "D", "text": "The embryo's total volume increases along with surface area, so the gain in exchange surface comes from overall growth rather than from a higher surface-area-to-volume ratio."}]$op14$::jsonb, 'C', $ex14$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Early Embryonic Development'. The answer is C because cleavage subdivides a fixed-volume embryo into ever-smaller cells, and smaller cells have a higher surface-area-to-volume ratio, increasing exchange surface per unit cytoplasm without any net growth. OpenStax Biology states that in cleavage "the egg is subdivided by successive cleavages into smaller and smaller cells, with no actual cell growth," and notes that "the 8-cell stage and the blastula are about the same size as the original zygote," so total volume is held constant while cell number rises. OpenStax Anatomy and Physiology reinforces this, noting that "Although each cleavage results in more cells, it does not increase the total volume of the conceptus" and that the cells "rapidly divide without changing the total volume." Geometrically, when a fixed volume is split into many smaller pieces, surface area scales with the square of linear size while volume scales with the cube, so as each blastomere shrinks its surface-area-to-volume ratio must rise, expanding the membrane available for nutrient, gas, and waste exchange relative to the cytoplasm each cell must service. (Choice A) is incorrect because shrinking cells gain, not lose, relative surface area; smaller cells have a higher surface-area-to-volume ratio, not a lower one. (Choice B) is incorrect because surface area and volume do not scale together when a cell halves: volume falls faster than surface area, so the ratio shifts upward rather than staying constant. (Choice C) is correct because subdividing a fixed volume into smaller blastomeres raises each cell's surface-area-to-volume ratio, buying more exchange membrane per unit cytoplasm with no change in total embryo volume. (Choice D) is incorrect because the text explicitly states cleavage occurs "with no actual cell growth" and "does not increase the total volume," so the added surface comes from subdivision, not from the embryo getting larger. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because it requires applying the square-cube scaling relationship to a described developmental scenario to predict how a measurable ratio changes and to infer the functional payoff, rather than recalling a stated fact.$ex14$, 'hard', '2C', $cc14$Early Embryonic Development$cc14$, 'biology', 2, 160
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'reversed_relationship', $a14A$Inverts the direction of the surface-area-to-volume change, claiming the ratio falls as cells shrink$a14A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'B', 'misconception', $a14B$Assumes surface area and volume scale linearly together, so the ratio is conserved across divisions$a14B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'partial_truth', $a14D$Correctly links more surface to exchange but wrongly attributes it to overall growth, contradicting the fixed-volume rule of cleavage$a14D$ FROM q;

-- Q15 [Blastulation | easy | Skill 1 | ans D]
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'Embryogenesis & Development', $sub15$Blastulation$sub15$, $qt15$During pre-embryonic development, a fertilized egg divides into a roughly 100-cell blastocyst in which one population of cells is set apart inside the structure while another forms the outer shell. A researcher tracking these cells predicts which population will give rise to the embryo proper. The population the researcher should identify is:$qt15$, $op15$[{"label": "A", "text": "the trophoblast, the outer shell of cells, which is fated to become the embryo proper"}, {"label": "B", "text": "the blastocoel, the fluid-filled internal cavity, which is fated to become the embryo proper"}, {"label": "C", "text": "the syncytiotrophoblast, the fused outer trophoblast layer, which is fated to become the embryo proper"}, {"label": "D", "text": "the inner cell mass, the cluster of cells set apart inside the blastocyst, which is fated to become the embryo proper"}]$op15$::jsonb, 'D', $ex15$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Early Embryonic Development'. The answer is D because the inner cell mass is the cluster of cells set apart inside the blastocyst that is fated to become the embryo, while the cells of the outer shell form the trophoblast that becomes the fetal membranes and placenta. The text states that once the conceptus organizes around the blastocoel, "a group of cells forms into an inner cell mass, which is fated to become the embryo," and separately that "the cells that form the outer shell are called trophoblasts," which "develop into the chorionic sac and the fetal portion of the placenta." The glossary reinforces this by defining the inner cell mass as the "cluster of cells within the blastocyst that is fated to become the embryo." Thus the embryo proper derives specifically from the inner cell mass, not from the outer or cavity components. (Choice A) The trophoblast is the outer shell, but its fate is the chorionic sac and the fetal portion of the placenta, not the embryo proper, so its fate is reversed here. (Choice B) The blastocoel is the fluid-filled cavity, not a cell population, and a cavity cannot differentiate into the embryo. (Choice C) The syncytiotrophoblast is a real structure formed when superficial trophoblast cells fuse after implantation, but it digests endometrial cells to anchor the blastocyst and is not the source of the embryo. (Choice D) The inner cell mass is correct because it is the internal cluster of cells the text explicitly identifies as fated to become the embryo. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because it requires recalling the established fate of a defined cell population (the inner cell mass) within the mammalian blastocyst.$ex15$, 'easy', '2C', $cc15$Early Embryonic Development$cc15$, 'biology', 1, 75
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'reversed_relationship', $a15A$Swaps the fates of the two blastocyst cell populations, assigning the embryo-proper fate to the outer trophoblast instead of the inner cell mass$a15A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'B', 'misconception', $a15B$Treats the fluid-filled cavity (blastocoel) rather than a cell group as the source of the embryo$a15B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'adjacent_fact', $a15C$Names a real post-implantation trophoblast derivative (syncytiotrophoblast) that anchors the blastocyst but does not form the embryo$a15C$ FROM q;

-- Q16 [Blastulation | easy | Skill 1 | ans C]
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'Embryogenesis & Development', $sub16$Blastulation$sub16$, $qt16$A free-floating conceptus has divided into a solid, tightly packed ball of roughly 100 cells. Over the next day a researcher observes that this solid ball is converted into a hollow structure. Which structural change has most directly produced this hollow form?$qt16$, $op16$[{"label": "A", "text": "A primitive gut cavity, the archenteron, opens as cells migrate inward, hollowing out the solid ball."}, {"label": "B", "text": "An amniotic cavity opens between the inner cell mass and the trophoblast, hollowing out the solid ball."}, {"label": "C", "text": "A fluid-filled cavity, the blastocoel, opens within the cell mass, hollowing out the solid ball."}, {"label": "D", "text": "A yolk sac forms beneath the cell mass, hollowing out the solid ball."}]$op16$::jsonb, 'C', $ex16$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Early Embryonic Development'. The answer is C because blastulation is defined by the appearance of a fluid-filled internal cavity, the blastocoel, that converts the solid morula into a hollow blastula (blastocyst). The Anatomy & Physiology text states that the ball of "now tightly bound cells starts to secrete fluid and organize themselves around a fluid-filled cavity, the blastocoel," and that "at this developmental stage, the conceptus is referred to as a blastocyst." The Biology text confirms the same sequence, noting that "a solid morula is formed, followed by a hollow structure called a blastula." Thus the single event that hollows the solid ball is the opening of the blastocoel within the cell mass. (Choice A) The archenteron is the primitive gut cavity, but it forms during gastrulation when cells move inward, not during blastulation. (Choice B) The amniotic cavity is a real space that opens between the inner cell mass and the trophoblast, but only later, during the second week after implantation, so it does not produce the blastula. (Choice C) The blastocoel is the fluid-filled cavity whose appearance defines the blastula/blastocyst stage, making it the correct answer. (Choice D) The yolk sac is a real extra-embryonic membrane that forms later from the hypoblast and does not create the blastocoel. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because it requires recalling that the defining structural event of blastulation is the formation of the blastocoel cavity.$ex16$, 'easy', '2C', $cc16$Early Embryonic Development$cc16$, 'biology', 1, 70
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'process_step_confusion', $a16A$Attributing a later gastrulation structure (archenteron) to the blastulation step$a16A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'B', 'adjacent_fact', $a16B$Substituting a real later post-implantation cavity (amniotic) for the blastocoel$a16B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'adjacent_fact', $a16D$Substituting a real later extraembryonic membrane (yolk sac) for the blastocoel$a16D$ FROM q;

-- Q17 [Blastulation | medium | Skill 2 | ans A]
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'Embryogenesis & Development', $sub17$Blastulation$sub17$, $qt17$A human blastocyst floating in the uterus has organized into an inner cell mass and an outer trophoblast surrounding a fluid-filled blastocoel. A researcher labels one layer to track which descendants will form the chorion and the fetal portion of the placenta that exchanges nutrients and wastes with the parent. Which layer should the researcher label, and why?$qt17$, $op17$[{"label": "A", "text": "The outer trophoblast layer, because it develops into the chorion and the fetal part of the placenta that exchange nutrients and wastes with the parent."}, {"label": "B", "text": "The inner cell mass, because it develops into the chorion and the fetal part of the placenta that exchange nutrients with the parent."}, {"label": "C", "text": "The outer trophoblast layer, because it differentiates into the embryo's three germ layers and its body tissues."}, {"label": "D", "text": "The blastomeres of the solid morula, because they collectively develop into the placenta before any inner cell mass forms."}]$op17$::jsonb, 'A', $ex17$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Early Embryonic Development'. The answer is A because the outer trophoblast layer of the blastocyst, not the inner cell mass, is the cell population fated to form the chorionic sac and the fetal portion of the placenta. As the blastocyst forms, the cells separate into two fates: the inner cell mass "is fated to become the embryo," while the trophoblasts "will develop into the chorionic sac and the fetal portion of the placenta (the organ of nutrient, waste, and gas exchange between a pregnant person and the developing offspring)." Because the researcher wants to track the chorion- and placenta-forming descendants, the correct layer to label is the outer trophoblast. (Choice A) Correct: the trophoblast is the outer layer whose descendants build the chorion and the fetal part of the placenta. (Choice B) Incorrect: this reverses the fates, assigning the placenta-forming role to the inner cell mass, which actually becomes the embryo. (Choice C) Incorrect: it names the right layer but gives it the inner cell mass's fate, since the trophoblast does not form the embryo's germ layers and body tissues. (Choice D) Incorrect: the morula is an earlier solid mass of blastomeres, and the inner cell mass and trophoblast do not exist until the blastocyst forms, so no layer can produce the placenta "before" the inner cell mass. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because it requires mapping a structural target (chorion and fetal placenta) onto the correct blastocyst layer by reasoning from each layer's developmental fate rather than recalling a single labeled fact.$ex17$, 'medium', '2C', $cc17$Early Embryonic Development$cc17$, 'biology', 2, 105
  ) RETURNING id
)
