-- Q18 [Blastulation | medium | Skill 2 | ans B]
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'Embryogenesis & Development', $sub18$Blastulation$sub18$, $qt18$A developmental biologist measures the total volume of a sea urchin embryo at the single-celled zygote stage, the 8-cell stage, and the early blastula stage, and notes that the embryo is enclosed in a fertilization envelope that prevents any uptake of material from the surrounding seawater. She predicts the relationship between total embryo volume and the rising number of cells across these stages. Which prediction is best supported by how cleavage divisions partition the embryo?$qt18$, $op18$[{"label": "A", "text": "Total embryo volume roughly doubles at each cleavage, because each blastomere grows back to the zygote's size between successive divisions."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Total embryo volume stays about the same as the zygote, because the fixed cytoplasm is subdivided into progressively smaller blastomeres without growth."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Total embryo volume rises in proportion to cell number, because each blastomere remains the same size as the original zygote as the cells accumulate."}, {"label": "D", "text": "Total embryo volume falls to a small fraction of the zygote's, because the blastomeres shrink so steeply that the embryo loses net volume by the blastula stage."}]$op18$::jsonb, 'B', $ex18$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Early Embryonic Development'. The answer is B because cleavage is a series of mitotic divisions that subdivides the fixed cytoplasm of the zygote into smaller and smaller cells without any actual cell growth, so the whole embryo stays about the size of the original zygote even as the cell number climbs. The text states that "the egg is subdivided by successive cleavages into smaller and smaller cells, with no actual cell growth" and that "the cells divide without an increase in mass; that is, one large single-celled zygote divides into multiple smaller cells," and it explicitly notes that "the 8-cell stage and the blastula are about the same size as the original zygote." Because the fertilization envelope blocks any uptake of new material, no external mass is added, so the total volume is essentially conserved while it is partitioned among more and more blastomeres. This is why cleavage differs from ordinary somatic mitosis, in which a cell grows back to full size before each division. (Choice A) is wrong because blastomeres do not grow back between divisions; the text specifies division occurs "with no actual cell growth," so volume does not double each cleavage. (Choice B) is correct because the cytoplasm is merely subdivided into progressively smaller blastomeres without growth, keeping total volume near that of the zygote. (Choice C) is wrong because the blastomeres do not stay zygote-sized; they become "smaller and smaller," so total volume does not scale up with cell count. (Choice D) correctly notes that the cells shrink but wrongly predicts a net loss of volume, contradicting the statement that the 8-cell stage and blastula are "about the same size as the original zygote." This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because it requires combining the constraint that no external material can enter with the partitioning mechanism of cleavage to predict how a measured quantity, total embryo volume, behaves across developmental stages.$ex18$, 'medium', '2C', $cc18$Early Embryonic Development$cc18$, 'biology', 2, 110
  ) RETURNING id
)
