-- Q19 [Blastulation | hard | Skill 2 | ans D]
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'Embryogenesis & Development', $sub19$Blastulation$sub19$, $qt19$A comparative embryologist studies two species: a placental mammal whose egg contains very little yolk, and a bird whose egg is densely packed with yolk. After fertilization, she predicts that only the mammalian embryo will develop into a true hollow ball of cells surrounding a fluid-filled blastocoel. Which feature of the mammalian egg best accounts for this predicted outcome?$qt19$, $op19$[{"label": "A", "text": "Its eggs are packed with yolk, so cleavage is partial (meroblastic) and the cells form only a surface layer over an undivided yolk mass instead of a hollow ball."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Its eggs hold very little yolk, so cleavage stops at the solid morula and never hollows into a blastocoel because there is no fluid source."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Its eggs hold very little yolk, so each cleavage adds cytoplasm and the embryo enlarges as it cleaves, producing a large hollow blastocyst bigger than the zygote."}, {"label": "D", "text": "Its eggs hold very little yolk, so the entire egg cleaves completely (holoblastic) and the cells can organize into a true hollow blastocyst around a blastocoel."}]$op19$::jsonb, 'D', $ex19$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Early Embryonic Development'. The answer is D because the amount of yolk in the egg determines the cleavage pattern, and only complete (holoblastic) cleavage of a yolk-poor egg can yield a fully hollow blastula. The text states that "The type of cleavage depends on the amount of yolk in the eggs," and that placental mammals, whose "eggs have a very small amount of yolk," "undergo holoblastic cleavage," while yolk-rich bird eggs "undergo meroblastic" (partial) cleavage. Because the entire mammalian egg divides, the blastomeres can "organize themselves around a fluid-filled cavity, the blastocoel," forming the blastocyst; the text notes the blastula is "hollow only in" species "whose eggs have relatively small amounts of yolk," whereas in "very yolky eggs" the "yolk remains undivided, with most cells forming an embryonic layer on the surface of the yolk." (Choice A) is reversed: it assigns the bird's yolk-rich, meroblastic, surface-layer pattern to the mammal, contradicting that the low-yolk mammalian egg cleaves completely. (Choice B) correctly notes low yolk and holoblastic cleavage but wrongly denies blastocoel formation, since the tightly bound cells secrete fluid and organize around the blastocoel to become the blastocyst. (Choice C) is wrong because cleavage subdivides the egg "with no actual cell growth" and "does not increase the total volume of the conceptus," so the blastula is about the same size as the zygote, not larger. (Choice D) is correct because complete (holoblastic) cleavage of the yolk-poor egg permits the cells to form a true hollow blastocyst surrounding a blastocoel. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because it requires using the yolk-amount-to-cleavage-pattern relationship to predict and explain a difference in blastula geometry between two species rather than simply recalling a definition.$ex19$, 'hard', '2C', $cc19$Early Embryonic Development$cc19$, 'biology', 2, 150
  ) RETURNING id
)
