-- Q22 [Implantation & Extraembryonic Membranes | medium | Skill 2 | ans C]
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'Embryogenesis & Development', $sub22$Implantation & Extraembryonic Membranes$sub22$, $qt22$A 3-week-old human embryo is generating its first circulating erythrocytes well before any bone marrow or fetal liver has formed. A teratogen then selectively destroys one extraembryonic membrane, and the embryo loses its capacity to produce these earliest blood cells. Which membrane was destroyed?$qt22$, $op22$[{"label": "A", "text": "The allantois, because this outpocketing handles early fluid and blood-cell production before the marrow forms"}, {"label": "B", "text": "The amnion, because the membranous sac that fills with fluid also generates the embryo's first circulating blood cells"}, {"label": "C", "text": "The yolk sac, because this membrane is the first site of blood-cell formation in the early embryo"}, {"label": "D", "text": "The chorion, because the outermost membrane that surrounds all the others is where the embryo's first blood cells arise"}]$op22$::jsonb, 'C', $ex22$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Early Embryonic Development'. The answer is C because the yolk sac is the first tissue to carry out hemopoiesis in the developing embryo, supplying the earliest blood cells before the fetal liver, spleen, and red bone marrow take over. The text states that "hemopoiesis occurs in a number of tissues, beginning with the yolk sac of the developing embryo" and that once the placenta assumes nourishment around week 4, the yolk sac's "main function is to serve as the source of blood cells and germ cells." Because the scenario destroys whichever membrane is responsible for the embryo's earliest blood-cell production, only the loss of the yolk sac fits that role; the other membranes have non-hematopoietic functions. (Choice A) The allantois is a finger-like outpocketing of the yolk sac that serves as "a primitive excretory duct of the embryo that will become part of the urinary bladder," not a hematopoietic site, so destroying it would not abolish early blood-cell formation. (Choice B) The amnion is a fluid-filled sac whose role is protective—the embryo "is protected from trauma and rapid temperature changes"—and it does not generate blood cells. (Choice C) The yolk sac is correct because it is the first source of the embryo's blood cells before the marrow exists. (Choice D) The chorion is "the one membrane that surrounds all others," forming chorionic villi that "make up the fetal portion of the placenta," so its role is placental exchange rather than early hematopoiesis. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because it requires interpreting an experimental loss-of-function scenario and tracing a specific defect back to the one membrane whose function is early blood-cell production.$ex22$, 'medium', '2C', $cc22$Early Embryonic Development$cc22$, 'biology', 2, 105
  ) RETURNING id
)
