-- Q23 [Implantation & Extraembryonic Membranes | hard | Skill 2 | ans D]
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'Embryogenesis & Development', $sub23$Implantation & Extraembryonic Membranes$sub23$, $qt23$During placental development, the chorionic villi extend from the chorionic membrane and burrow into the endometrium, where maternal blood pools around them. A physiologist tracks how oxygen and dissolved nutrients reach the fetus and confirms that no maternal red blood cells are ever recovered from fetal vessels. Which account best predicts how substances reach the fetal circulation given this architecture?$qt23$, $op23$[{"label": "A", "text": "Maternal and fetal blood pool together inside each chorionic villus, letting whole maternal red blood cells pass directly so that oxygen is handed off cell-to-cell."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Exchange takes place across the amnion, where the fluid-filled sac enclosing the fetus serves as the barrier that maternal nutrients diffuse across into fetal blood."}, {"label": "C", "text": "The villi actively pump maternal blood cells into the fetal circulation, so oxygen, glucose, and iron all cross together by one shared active-transport route."}, {"label": "D", "text": "The villi present a large surface bathed in maternal blood, and substances diffuse across the villus wall into fetal capillaries inside, while the two bloodstreams never directly mix."}]$op23$::jsonb, 'D', $ex23$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Early Embryonic Development'. The answer is D because the chorionic villi 'burrow into the endometrium like tree roots' and create a large interface where maternal blood surrounds the villi while fetal blood runs through capillaries inside them, so substances cross the villus wall while the two bloodstreams stay separate. The text states that 'Nutrients and oxygen are transferred from maternal blood surrounding the villi through the capillaries and into the fetal bloodstream' (A&P 57835-57836), and that 'Maternal and fetal blood does not commingle because blood cells cannot move across the placenta' (A&P 57841). Transfer therefore depends on substances moving across the villus barrier, not on the bloodstreams contacting each other, which is exactly why no maternal red blood cells appear in fetal vessels. (Choice A) is incorrect because maternal and fetal blood 'does not commingle' and 'blood cells cannot move across the placenta,' so whole red cells never pass and oxygen is not delivered cell-to-cell. (Choice B) is incorrect because gas and nutrient exchange occurs at the chorionic villi, whereas the amnion is the membranous sac that 'fills with amniotic fluid' to surround the embryo (A&P 57734) rather than serving as the exchange barrier. (Choice C) is incorrect because only amino acids and iron cross by active transport while oxygen crosses by simple diffusion and glucose by facilitated diffusion (A&P 57837-57839), and blood cells do not cross at all. (Choice D) is correct because diffusion across the broad villus surface into fetal capillaries lets substances move between the two compartments even though maternal and fetal blood never directly mix. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because it requires reasoning from the structural arrangement of the villi and the no-mixing constraint to predict the mechanism by which exchange must occur.$ex23$, 'hard', '2C', $cc23$Early Embryonic Development$cc23$, 'biology', 2, 160
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
