UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'partial_truth', $a23C$Overgeneralizing active transport (true only for amino acids and iron) to all substances and to blood cells$a23C$ FROM q;

-- Q24 [Implantation & Extraembryonic Membranes | hard | Skill 2 | ans A]
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'Embryogenesis & Development', $sub24$Implantation & Extraembryonic Membranes$sub24$, $qt24$Midway through a pregnancy, a chronic rupture of the membrane directly enclosing the fetus drains away most of the surrounding fluid, leaving the fetus pressed against the wall of the cavity. A clinician predicts which protective function has been most directly lost. Which prediction is best supported?$qt24$, $op24$[{"label": "A", "text": "The fetus loses its cushioning against mechanical trauma and rapid temperature change and its room to move, because the lost fluid was held by the amnion"}, {"label": "B", "text": "The fetus loses its primitive excretory drainage, because the lost fluid was the route the allantois used to void early metabolic wastes"}, {"label": "C", "text": "The fetus loses its early nutrient supply and blood-cell source, because that fluid was produced by the yolk sac to nourish the conceptus"}, {"label": "D", "text": "The fetus loses the membrane that encloses all the others, because draining the fluid collapses the outermost protective chorion"}]$op24$::jsonb, 'A', $ex24$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Early Embryonic Development'. The answer is A because the amnion is the extra-embryonic membrane that fills with amniotic fluid and grows to surround the embryo, and it is that fluid that provides the protective functions in question. The text states that "The amnion fills with amniotic fluid and eventually grows to surround the embryo" and that, "Floating within the amniotic fluid, the embryo—and later, the fetus—is protected from trauma and rapid temperature changes. It can move freely within the fluid." Losing this fluid therefore strips away the cushioning against mechanical trauma, the buffering against rapid temperature change, and the space to move—functions tied specifically to the amnion, not to the yolk sac, allantois, or chorion. The named scenario (fluid loss) must be mapped to the correct membrane and its correct function rather than to a different membrane that performs a different job. (Choice A) is correct because the fluid that cushions the fetus and lets it move freely is the amniotic fluid held by the amnion. (Choice B) is wrong because the allantois is "a primitive excretory duct of the embryo," a membrane distinct from the fluid-filled amnion, so draining amniotic fluid does not remove an excretory drainage route. (Choice C) is wrong because the yolk sac supplies nutrients and serves as a "source of blood cells and germ cells," but it does not produce the cushioning amniotic fluid, so its functions are not what the fluid loss compromises. (Choice D) is wrong because although the chorion is "the one membrane that surrounds all others," the shock-absorbing fluid that protects against trauma is held by the amnion, not the chorion, so the lost protective function is misattributed. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because it requires reading a clinical scenario of fluid loss, identifying which membrane holds the fluid, and predicting which specific protective function is compromised while ruling out functions belonging to other membranes.$ex24$, 'hard', '2C', $cc24$Early Embryonic Development$cc24$, 'biology', 2, 150
  ) RETURNING id
)
