-- Q25 [Implantation & Extraembryonic Membranes | medium | Skill 2 | ans D]
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'Embryogenesis & Development', $sub25$Implantation & Extraembryonic Membranes$sub25$, $qt25$During the third week of human development, a finger-like outpocketing buds off the yolk sac and forms a primitive excretory duct that stores the embryo's early nitrogenous wastes and will later become part of the urinary bladder. Which extraembryonic membrane has this embryologist identified?$qt25$, $op25$[{"label": "A", "text": "The amnion, the membranous sac that fills with fluid and encloses the developing fetus"}, {"label": "B", "text": "The chorion, the outermost membrane that surrounds all of the other extraembryonic membranes"}, {"label": "C", "text": "The yolk sac, which supplies early nutrients and serves as the source of the embryo's first blood cells"}, {"label": "D", "text": "The allantois, the finger-like outpocketing of the yolk sac that becomes a precursor to the urinary bladder"}]$op25$::jsonb, 'D', $ex25$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Early Embryonic Development'. The answer is D because the structure described—a week-3, finger-like outpocketing of the yolk sac that acts as a primitive excretory duct, stores nitrogenous waste, and becomes part of the bladder—is by definition the allantois. OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e states, "During week 3, a finger-like outpocketing of the yolk sac develops into the allantois, a primitive excretory duct of the embryo that will become part of the urinary bladder" (A&P 2e, lines 57758-57759). The glossary reinforces that the allantois is the "finger-like outpocketing of yolk sac" that "forms the primitive excretory duct of the embryo; precursor to the urinary bladder" (A&P 2e, lines 57754-57756). OpenStax Biology 2e independently confirms that "The allantois stores nitrogenous wastes produced by the embryo and also facilitates respiration" (Biology 2e, line 37566), matching the waste-storage clue. Each of the three clues in the stem therefore points uniquely to the allantois rather than to any other membrane. (Choice A) The amnion is the "transparent membranous sac that encloses the developing fetus and fills with amniotic fluid" (A&P 2e, lines 57733-57734), a protective fluid sac—not an excretory duct that becomes the bladder. (Choice B) The chorion is "the one membrane that surrounds all others" (A&P 2e, line 57762) and gives rise to the placenta, not the urinary bladder. (Choice C) The yolk sac is the membrane the allantois buds from; it "provides primitive blood circulation" and serves "as the source of blood cells and germ cells" (A&P 2e, lines 57755-57757), so it is the parent structure, not the excretory outpocketing itself. (Choice D) is correct because the allantois is the week-3 finger-like outpocketing of the yolk sac that serves as the embryo's primitive excretory duct and becomes part of the urinary bladder, satisfying all three clues. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because it requires integrating three independent clues—developmental timing, excretory function, and the eventual fate as bladder tissue—and mapping that converging evidence onto the single membrane whose definition satisfies all three.$ex25$, 'medium', '2C', $cc25$Early Embryonic Development$cc25$, 'biology', 2, 105
  ) RETURNING id
)
